ctl commands.\n\nWait, the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. So perhaps I should use that to get the list of pods and deployments in the social-network namespace. That would give a clearer picture of what\'s actually running. For example, running "kubectl get pods -n social-network" and "kubectl get deployments -n social-network" would show the current state of the workloads. If some pods are not running or in a crash loop, that could indicate issues. Also, checking the number of replicas and their status would help identify if there are any failures in the deployments.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the get_services tool isn\'t returning the correct data. But since the user already tried get_services and got a list, but the metrics for user-service are down, it\'s possible that the service isn\'t running. So, using kubectl to check the actual pods and deployments would be more reliable. That way, I can verify if the services are deployed and running as expected. If there are missing pods or deployments, that could be the root cause. So the next step is to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the pods and deployments in the namespace.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network"  \nTo verify the current state of pods in the `social-network` namespace, ensuring we identify any non-running or crash-looping pods that might indicate faults. This directly reveals the runtime state of workloads.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 500, 'prompt_tokens': 1661, 'total_tokens': 2161, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbea-e30f-76a0-8590-d7c53dad9830-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1661, 'output_tokens': 500, 'total_tokens': 216