ands, they can\'t use "kubectl logs -f" or similar.\n\nSo, the next step should be to get the list of all pods in the social-network namespace to identify the exact names of the pods. They already ran "kubectl get pods" but maybe they need to list all pods to find the specific one they want to check. Once they have the pod names, they can then use "kubectl logs" with the specific pod name to retrieve the logs without causing an error.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they should check the metrics again to see if any services are not functioning properly. For example, the earlier query for "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" returned an empty result, which might indicate that the user-service is not healthy. But since the deployment shows it\'s ready, there might be a discrepancy. They could check other metrics like error rates or latency using Prometheus.\n\nAnother approach is to look into the dependency graph using Jaeger to see if there are any service calls that are failing or having high latency. If there\'s a service that\'s not responding, it might be causing cascading issues. They could also check the traces for the user-service to see if there are any errors in the RPC calls.\n\nBut since the user is focusing on the user-service, maybe they should first confirm the pod name and then check the logs. However, since the previous attempt to get logs failed due to missing pod name, they need to list all pods first. Let me check the available tools again. The "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd" can be used with "kubectl get pods" to list all pods, which would help in identifying the correct pod name for the user-service. Once they have the pod name, they can then retrieve the logs. So the next tool call should be to get the list of pods again to find the exact name.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a217c7fdc09eb164', 'type': 'funct