r. But the available tools don\'t include getting deployment YAMLs. So perhaps using get_metrics to check the up status of all services.\n\nWait, the user already checked user-service\'s up metric and got an empty result. That\'s a red flag. So maybe the user-service is not up, but the deployment shows it\'s available. That could mean that the pods are running but not healthy. So the next step is to check the metrics for user-service again, or check other services. Alternatively, check the metrics for the Jaeger service to see if it\'s up. But the user hasn\'t checked that yet. Alternatively, use get_traces for a service to see if there are any errors in the traces. For example, if user-service is down, traces might show errors. But without knowing which service is faulty, it\'s hard to say.\n\nAlternatively, check the get_dependency_graph to see if there are any services that are not connected properly. But the user hasn\'t done that yet. The next logical step is to check the metrics for all services to identify which ones are not up. However, the available tools don\'t have a way to list all services except get_services. Wait, the user already used get_services, but the response wasn\'t provided. Wait, in the first step, the user called get_services, but the response wasn\'t shown. Wait, looking back, the user first called get_services, then get_metrics for user-service, then checked deployments and pods. The get_services tool returns a list of service names, which the user hasn\'t used yet. Maybe the user should first get the list of services from Grafana using get_services, then check their metrics. But the user hasn\'t done that yet. So maybe the next step is to call get_services to get the list of services, then check each service\'s up metric. But the user might not have the list of services yet. Alternatively, check the metrics for all known services. But the user might not know which services to check. \n\nAlternatively, since the user-service\'s up metric 