other services yet. \n\nAlternatively, the user could use get_dependency_graph to see if there are any services that are not properly connected, which might indicate a fault. But the user hasn\'t done that yet. \n\nConsidering the previous steps, the user has already checked deployments and pods, and the only pending pod is Jaeger. However, the user-service\'s up metric is empty, which is a critical issue. So the next step is to check the metrics for user-service again, or check the get_traces for user-service to see if there are any errors. But the user can\'t log into the pods, so they can\'t check the logs. \n\nAlternatively, the user could use get_metrics with a different query to check for errors or latency in user-service. For example, checking for error rates or request latencies. But the user hasn\'t done that yet. \n\nIn conclusion, the next step is to check the metrics for user-service again to confirm if it\'s down, or check the get_traces for user-service to see if there are any errors. But since the user can\'t log into the pods, they need to rely on metrics and traces. So the next tool call should be get_metrics for user-service again, or check the get_traces for user-service.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics  \n"up{job=\\"user-service\\"}"  \nTo verify the health status of the `user-service` by checking if it\'s up and running. The previous query returned an empty result, indicating potential issues with the service\'s availability. This step confirms whether the service is truly down or if there\'s a metric scraping problem.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1685, 'prompt_tokens': 3733, 'total_tokens': 5418, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbeb-9dd6-7dd1-a74e-e38593b7572e-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3733, 'output_tokens': 1685, 'total_tokens': 5418}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3b029133-8f6e-4461-bd35-4371a3ac5e5b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a social network application composed of many microservices. They\'ve already checked the deployments and pods, and everything seems to be running. But the initial metric query for the user-service returned no results, which is confusing because the pod is listed as running.\n\nHmm, maybe the problem is with the metric scraping configuration. If the Prometheus instance isn\'t scraping the user-service\'s metrics, the up{} query would return empty. I should check the metrics again, perhaps with a more specific query or a different approach. Wait, the user already tried "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" and got an empty result. Maybe the job label isn\'t correctly set in the service\'s Prometheus exporter. Alternatively, there could be a network issue preventing Prometheus from reaching the service\'s metrics endpoint.\n\nAnother angle: since the pod is running, but the metrics aren\'t being scraped, maybe the service\'s exporter isn\'t exposed correctly. I should check the service\'s configuration to ensure it\'s exposing metrics on the right port and that the Prometheus service is configured to scrape it. But since I can\'t directly check the service config, maybe I can use the get_metrics tool again with a different query to see if any related metrics are available. For example, checking for CPU or memory usage might help determine if the service is actually running and exporting metrics. If those metrics are also missing, it points to a configuration issue. If they are present, maybe the up{} query is misconfigured. Alternatively, maybe the user-service isn\'t actually running, but the pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state, but the previous kubectl get pods showed it as Running. This is a bit conflicting. I need to verify the metrics again to confirm if the service is up or if there\'s a scraping problem. Let me try querying for the up metric again, maybe with a different job name or label. If that doesn\'t work, perhaps check other metrics to see if there\'s any data. If no metrics are present, the issue is likely with the service\'s exporter or Prometheus configuration. If metrics are present, then the up{} query might be incorrect. But given the previous result, I\'ll proceed to check the metrics again with the same query to confirm.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8a634323c49a0b1e', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 500, 'prompt_tokens': 4706, 'total_tokens': 5206, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbeb-ec62-7523-b7b3-61d152329d99-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'up{job="user-service"}'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8a634323c49a0b1e', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4706, 'output_tokens': 500, 'total_tokens': 5206}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='a8e6cf60-b261-46cd-b9fb-0c00ac0d4bf9', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8a634323c49a0b1e'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='54ea82cd-8c5e-43e7-8155-f9e6b155a77c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their social network application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments and pods, and everything seems to be running. But the user-service isn\'t showing up in the metrics. The get_metrics query for up{job="user-service"} returned an empty result. That suggests that the user-service might not be scraping metrics, or maybe the Prometheus job isn\'t configured correctly.\n\nWait, the user mentioned that the application uses Thrift RPCs for communication. Maybe there\'s a problem with the service\'s ability to expose metrics. Since the previous check with kubectl showed the user-service pod is running, the issue might be with the metrics endpoint or the Prometheus configuration. \n\nI should check the service\'s configuration in Kubernetes. Maybe the user-service isn\'t set up to expose metrics on the correct port. Alternatively, the Prometheus job might not be targeting the correct endpoint. Another angle: maybe the service is using a different job name in Prometheus. Let me check the Prometheus configuration for the user-service. But since I can\'t directly access the config, perhaps using the get_metrics tool again with a different query could help. Or maybe check the service\'s endpoints to see if the metrics are being exposed. \n\nWait, the user has the get_services tool which lists services in Grafana. Maybe checking the services available there could show if the user-service is properly registered. Alternatively, using get_operations to see what operations are available for the user-service. But the main issue is the missing metrics. \n\nAnother approach: check the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors related to metrics exposure. But the previous attempt to get logs failed because the pod name wasn\'t specified. The user tried to get logs with a placeholder pod name, which isn\'t valid. So, I need to first get the exact pod name for user-service. \n\nLooking back, the kubectl get pods output shows user-service-559b79c875-6pwjf is running. So, the pod name is user-service-559b79c875-6pwjf. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool, I can get the logs for that specific pod. But the user tried that before and got an error because they used a placeholder. Now that I have the actual pod name, I can try again. \n\nWait, the user tried to run kubectl logs user-service-<pod-name> but got a command rejected error. Maybe the tool doesn\'t support dynamic pod names. Alternatively, maybe the logs aren\'t available due to the service not exposing them. Alternatively, the issue is with the metrics configuration. \n\nAlternatively, check the service\'s deployment to see if the container is configured to expose metrics. For example, if the user-service is using a sidecar container for metrics, or if the main container is not set up to expose /metrics. \n\nBut given the tools available, the next step is to check the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors. So, using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the specific pod name. Let me try that. If that\'s not possible, maybe check the service\'s configuration in Kubernetes, like the deployment or service YAML files. But the user might not have access to those. \n\nAlternatively, check the Prometheus targets to see if the user-service is listed as a target. But the get_metrics tool is for querying, not checking targets. \n\nHmm. The user\'s main issue is the missing metrics for user-service. Since the pod is running, the problem is likely in the metrics setup. So, checking the logs for any errors related to metrics exposure would be the next step. Let me proceed with that.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-6pwjf -n social-network"  \nTo inspect the logs of the specific `user-service` pod to identify any runtime errors or issues preventing it from exposing metrics or functioning correctly. This will help determine if the service is crashing, has configuration errors, or is otherwise unresponsive.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 858, 'prompt_tokens': 3904, 'total_tokens': 4762, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbec-0519-72b3-9e0f-dcb12d56b5b9-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3904, 'output_tokens': 858, 'total_tokens': 4762}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c85892f4-1022-4446-acd6-07262cf3a47a'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the Social Network application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they've checked the deployments and pods, and everything seems to be running. But when they checked the metrics for the user-service, the query returned an empty result, which is concerning.\n\nThe user tried to get the logs for the user-service pod, but the tool rejected the command because they didn't specify the pod name. Wait, in the previous step, the user tried to use `kubectl logs user-service-<pod-name>` but got an error. The correct approach would be to first get the list of pods for the user-service to find the exact pod name. \n\nLooking back, the output from `kubectl get pods -n social-network` shows a pod named `user-service-559b79c875-6pwjf`. So the correct command should use that specific pod name. The user might have tried to use a placeholder like `<pod-name>` instead of the actual name. \n\nTherefore, the next step should be to execute `kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-6pwjf -n social-network` to retrieve the logs. This will help identify any errors or issues within the user-service pod that might be causing it to not report metrics or function correctly. If the logs show errors, that could point to the root cause, such as a configuration mistake, a crash, or a problem with the service's dependencies.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-6pwjf -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-85a638538368c3aa', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 371, 'prompt_tokens': 4890, 'total_tokens': 5261, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbec-2d04-7663-a1f6-4b55260c1fc4-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-6pwjf -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-85a638538368c3aa', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4890, 'output_tokens': 371, 'total_tokens': 5261}), ToolMessage(content="[2026-Mar-17 13:08:34.152308] <info>: (UserHandler.h:932:GetMachineId) MAC address = 92:2a:65:64:03:7b\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:34.152486] <info>: (UserService.cpp:63:main) machine_id = f44\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:34.453123] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelectionTimeoutMS` expired: [connection refused calling ismaster on 'user-mongodb:27017']\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:34.453608] <error>: (UserService.cpp:80:main) Failed to create mongodb index, try again\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:35.752080] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelectionTimeoutMS` expired: [connection refused calling ismaster on 'user-mongodb:27017']\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:35.752149] <error>: (UserService.cpp:80:main) Failed to create mongodb index, try again\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:37.051089] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelectionTimeoutMS` expired: [connection refused calling ismaster on 'user-mongodb:27017']\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:37.051178] <error>: (UserService.cpp:80:main) Failed to create mongodb index, try again\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:38.351153] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelectionTimeoutMS` expired: [connection refused calling ismaster on 'user-mongodb:27017']\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:38.351258] <error>: (UserService.cpp:80:main) Failed to create mongodb index, try again\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:39.650138] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelectionTimeoutMS` expired: [connection refused calling ismaster on 'user-mongodb:27017']\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:39.650253] <error>: (UserService.cpp:80:main) Failed to create mongodb index, try again\n[2026-Mar-17 13:08:43.124348] <info>: (UserService.cpp:94:main) Starting the user-service server ...\n", id='ec805263-4d14-4353-af56-c096c251bce1', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-85a638538368c3aa'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a4084a88-a016-455c-8fb5-f698616f8242'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is having issues connecting to the user-mongodb. The logs show repeated connection refused errors when trying to create an index. So the problem is likely with the MongoDB service.\n\nFirst, I need to check if the MongoDB service is running. The user-mongodb deployment should be up. Let me use kubectl to get the pods for user-mongodb. If the pod is not running, that\'s a problem. Also, check the service configuration to ensure the endpoints are correct.\n\nWait, the user-service is trying to connect to user-mongodb:27017. Maybe the MongoDB service isn\'t exposing the correct port or the pod isn\'t accessible. Let me check the service YAML for user-mongodb. Also, check if there are any network policies blocking the connection. Maybe a NetworkPolicy is restricting access. \n\nAlternatively, the MongoDB pod might be in a CrashLoopBackOff state. Let me check the logs of the user-mongodb pod to see if it\'s starting correctly. If the MongoDB pod isn\'t running, that\'s the root cause. Also, verify the PersistentVolume and PVC for user-mongodb to ensure the data directory is correctly mounted. If the storage is misconfigured, MongoDB might fail to start. \n\nAnother angle: the user-service might be using the wrong hostname or port for MongoDB. Check the environment variables in the user-service deployment to confirm the MongoDB connection string. If the service name is incorrect or the port is wrong, that would cause connection issues. \n\nSo, the next steps are to check the status of the user-mongodb pod, inspect its logs, verify the service configuration, and ensure there are no network policies blocking the connection. Let\'s start by checking the pods for user-mongodb.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network -l app=user-mongodb"  \nTo verify the status of the `user-mongodb` pod and check if it is running or in a crash loop, which would explain the connection refusal errors observed in the `user-service` logs. This directly addresses the root cause of the MongoDB connectivity issue.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 439, 'prompt_tokens': 4862, 'total_tokens': 5301, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbec-416f-7950-81c5-3cffb798378f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4862, 'output_tokens': 439, 'total_tokens': 5301}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8fc74783-67cc-4989-902b-b6298e0107bc'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user-service logs show connection refused errors when trying to connect to user-mongodb. So the next step is to check the status of the user-mongodb pod. I need to use kubectl to get the pods labeled with app=user-mongodb in the social-network namespace. That will tell me if the MongoDB pod is running or if there's an issue with it. If the pod isn't running, maybe it's crashing or there's a configuration problem. Also, checking the logs of the user-mongodb pod could help identify why it's not accepting connections. But first, I need to confirm the pod's status.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network -l app=user-mongodb"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-928ecb4640cf9152', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 171, 'prompt_tokens': 5842, 'total_tokens': 6013, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbec-565b-7c60-a520-96616cb89819-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n social-network -l app=user-mongodb'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-928ecb4640cf9152', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5842, 'output_tokens': 171, 'total_tokens': 6013}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\nuser-mongodb-6c75c9d7db-vswfl   1/1     Running   0          3m55s\n', id='8021b38a-cd39-4740-916a-30fbd1e23e8c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-928ecb4640cf9152'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5db60d6a-0f20-4891-8722-f8f4d82f258c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service is having issues connecting to user-mongodb. The logs show connection refused errors. The user-mongodb pod is running, so maybe the problem is with the MongoDB service itself or the network configuration.\n\nI should check the MongoDB service to make sure it\'s properly exposed. If the service isn\'t running or has the wrong endpoints, the user-service can\'t connect. Let me use kubectl to get the service details for user-mongodb. That way, I can verify the endpoints and ports are correct. If the service isn\'t pointing to the right pod, that would cause the connection issues. Also, checking the service\'s YAML might show if there are any misconfigurations. Once I confirm the service is set up correctly, maybe the next step is to check the MongoDB pod\'s logs for any errors. But first, let\'s verify the service configuration.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get service user-mongodb -n social-network"  \nTo verify the service configuration for `user-mongodb` and ensure it is correctly exposing the MongoDB instance. If the service is misconfigured (e.g., incorrect endpoints, ports, or selectors), the `user-service` would fail to connect, leading to the observed "connection refused" errors. This step confirms whether the service is properly routing traffic to the running MongoDB pod.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 280, 'prompt_tokens': 5078, 'total_tokens': 5358, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbec-617b-74b0-91f9-31b8799e9a15-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5078, 'output_tokens': 280, 'total_tokens': 5358}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b20b5e28-5aca-41ad-9d98-1fe08aee674b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user-service is having trouble connecting to user-mongodb, as indicated by the logs showing connection refused errors. The MongoDB pod itself is running, so the issue might be with how the service is set up.\n\nFirst, I checked the pods for user-mongodb and found that the pod is running. But the service might not be pointing to the correct pod. The next step is to check the service configuration. The service should have the right selector to target the user-mongodb pod and the correct port forwarding.\n\nSo, I need to use the kubectl get service command for user-mongodb in the social-network namespace. This will show the service's endpoints and ports. If the endpoints are not listed or are incorrect, that means the service isn't properly connected to the pod. Also, checking the target port and port settings is crucial to ensure that the service is listening on the right ports and forwarding traffic correctly.\n\nBy verifying the service configuration, I can determine if there's a misalignment between the service and the pod, which would cause the connection issues. If the service is misconfigured, that's the root cause. If everything looks good, maybe there's a network policy or firewall rule blocking the connection, but that's a bit more advanced. For now, checking the service is the logical next step.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get service user-mongodb -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b96f3a5d9f74a704', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 306, 'prompt_tokens': 6078, 'total_tokens': 6384, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbec-6ec8-74f0-8b41-6d8b99e27c8a-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get service user-mongodb -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b96f3a5d9f74a704', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6078, 'output_tokens': 306, 'total_tokens': 6384}), ToolMessage(content='NAME           TYPE        CLUSTER-IP      EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)     AGE\nuser-mongodb   ClusterIP   10.101.21.190   <none>        27017/TCP   4m6s\n', id='2786b96d-03c3-4c0d-a1c0-e54dec3d1dfb', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b96f3a5d9f74a704'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='89db313f-a88f-4690-a178-221b6b36019d')]]
